course: dictionary
lesson: 2976
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dryness is a noun. It means being dry, with little or no water or wetness. In «Cold and dryness stop rotting», the dryness is what keeps the old huts from decaying.
explain: Dryness can also describe the body. Dryness of skin, eyes or mouth means they feel dry and uncomfortable, like cracked lips after a long walk in the sun.
explain: Dryness of humour is different. It is a quiet, serious-faced way of being funny, so the joke hides behind a calm voice. Look at the words around dryness to tell which meaning the writer means: water, the body, or the funny kind.
fact: Dryness is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some dryness, not a dryness.
fact: «Dryness» means being dry, with little or no water or wetness.
fact: «Dryness» of skin, eyes or mouth means they feel dry and uncomfortable.
fact: «Dryness» of humour means a quiet, serious-faced way of being funny.
fact: The Svalbard Global Seed Vault in Norway keeps over a million seed samples safe; «cold and dryness slow a seed right down, so it stays alive for a very long time».
exercise: «Cold and dryness stop rotting» in the old huts. What does dryness mean here? | Being funny in a quiet way | A very loud noise | *Having little or no water or wetness | A feeling of being sleepy
exercise: Ella's lips feel cracked and tight after the desert walk. Which word names this problem? | Thickness | Darkness | *Dryness | Sweetness
exercise: Which phrase means a quiet, serious-faced way of being funny? | Skin dryness | Dryness of the soil | *Dryness of humour | Dryness of the air
test: «The seed vault keeps seeds alive because cold and dryness slow them right down.» What does dryness mean in that sentence? | A quiet, serious way of joking | *Having little or no water or wetness | Feeling uncomfortable in the mouth | Being very old
test: Which is the best example of dryness of skin? | A rain cloud over the sea | A cat purring on a warm rug | *Hands that feel rough and tight | A dog wagging its tail
exam: Ahmed reads «Scott's and Shackleton's huts still stand after over 100 years» and says dryness must mean rain. Which is right? | Yes, dryness is a wet storm | No, dryness is a kind of food | Yes, because huts always fall down in rain | *No, dryness means little or no water, so the cold and dry air stopped the huts rotting
